 course builds upon the differential calculus covered in Calculus I , providing in-depth coverage of integral calculus including integration techniques, applications of integration. Advanced topics such as series convergence tests, Taylor series, calculus with parametric and polar equations, and introductory differential equations are also covered. This comprehensive course will prepare students for further studies in advanced mathematics, engineering, statistics, machine learning, and other fields requiring a solid foundation in integral calculus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1009 "Further your understanding of calculus: master advanced integration techniques, model real-world situations using differential equations, and more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1012 106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1013 "CA2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "4965f9cc-fd21-5d50-b441-6e80983b17dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region Between Two Curves About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1033 3641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "aa7fefd1-2912-58e4-b2a0-bbf07ec60fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1033 415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1033 1084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186174681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab089e7c-7f52-5582-ac6f-752c4f8bf60f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174677 #edb/ref 1080 "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(4y - 2y^{2})dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174677 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174677 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ba2acd00-9b53-53b6-ab77-4f405beaee41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174678 #edb/ref 1080 "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(2y^{2} - 4y)^{2}dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d71365f0-76a4-5135-8feb-bb4b94aaac69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174679 #edb/ref 1080 "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(6y - 2y^{2})dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d8db48f2-8530-5ffc-b6fe-aa0cd877d5bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174680 #edb/ref 1080 "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(2y^{2} - 4y)dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174680 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174680 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da491968-8279-58a2-8f9e-4f73a8cbab30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174681 #edb/ref 1080 "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(4y - 2y^{2})^{2}dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36e48c5d-45b8-5f5e-8a05-a5164f6ff937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y$, as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7520ab43-c5b9-541b-80a9-4af1b91b2428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=3y-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n3y - y^{2} &= y \\\\\n2y - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny(2 - y) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y([3y - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y(2y - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}(2y^{2} - y^{3})dy \\\\\n&= 2π(2\\int_{0}^{2}y^{2}dy - \\int_{0}^{2}y^{3}dy) \\\\\n&= 2π(2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{2} - \\left.\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π(2(\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - (\\frac{16}{4} - 0)) \\\\\n&= 2π(\\frac{16}{3} - 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2$, $x=y$, and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fa/fa2a775faed9c425a3b94e417b761d11486b4f41dae3129d9c9e016c28a652f0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f7f3606-5c49-51d0-b4e5-cffd0310b6c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=2-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n2 - y^{2} &= y \\\\\ny^{2} + y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(y + 2)(y - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -2, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/4c/4cc38a21168a86d2b64289e9c7877fbff4bf976b7214ed4befeb9644894f6172.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y([2 - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y(2 - y - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region Between Two Curves About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d4bf6855-3ee9-564c-a98f-cca62f229eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x = 5y - 2y^{2}$ and $x = y$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2289657c-a9c3-56eb-97a8-f3d9729a0b73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1061 33079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e02ebde-56c8-5084-9591-4df36f3cd368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1061 33080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06aa8368-cb63-543a-ad96-6e6d78f9e484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1061 33083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f92ebc5-99c7-586a-a2b6-1b4a35a8f655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176983 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $x=f(y)$ on the right, $x=g(y)$ on the left, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$, as shown below. We assume that\n\n$$\nf(y) \\geq g(y)\n$$\n\nfor all $y \\in [c,d]$.\n\n![](images/9a/9a3970253f710223a77fe43f717df908fc20f27950744166ea7197897d64936d.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([f(y)] - [g(y)])dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176983 #edb/ref 1065 5293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176983 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee650309-6a00-514b-83cf-cc7028b14529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176983 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
